  "query_preview": "..\n\nLagged sub 700mb dry air advection will pick up throughout the \nmorning hours as flow trajectories now become increasingly \nanticyclonic. While not easily discernible in the surface \nobservations, a significant 850mb dewpoint gradient will work across \nthe area in the 12-15z time window that will be correlated with \nactive subsidence and deepening isentropic downglide. Rising 1000-\n500mb geopotential heights will support a stable surface anticyclone \nthat will build directly across Lower Michigan today and tonight. \nBelow normal temperatures despite full insolation with highs this \nafternoon near 60 degrees. Calm wind environment brings the \npotential for a good radiative cooling night tonight that could \nallow temperatures to drop down well into the 30s. One item of note \nis very strong warm advection that will occur immediately upstream \nof Lake Michigan overnight, where some warm advection cloud could \nsurvive and spill down towards Southeast Michigan. Probabilistic \noutput suggests probabilities of reaching less than 37 degrees is at \napproximately 30-40% only after 4am Tuesday morning. Will defer any \nfrost headlines to future shifts.\n\nThe warm advection arrives for Tuesday with no upper level jet \nforcing and no shortwave energy of note. Forecast soundings show \nvery poor moisture increasing with the warm front aloft that also \nexhibits very poor lapse rates. Preference is with a dry forecast \nfor the middle of the week as best synoptic scale lift support rides \nnorth of Lake Huron. Middle 60s for highs on Tuesday with highs then \nreaching the low 70s Wednesday. Trough wavelength increases \nsignificantly late Wednesday and Thursday that will essentially \ncarve out a cold front over or to the south of Lower Michigan. \nStatic stability is forecasted to remain relatively high above 10.0 \nkft agl despite some modest uptick in lower level thetae. Could see \nsome sprinkles or some accas/altocumulus Wednesday afternoon.\n\nPersistence forecast looks good for the end of the week with dry \nconditions and daytime temperatures reaching the upper 60s. A great \nstretch of prototypical Autumn weather before a significant ridging \nand warming potential occurs by Day 7 next Sunday.",
